= Introduction <sec:introduction>

In bottom-up proteomics, MS1 survey scans are conventionally the quantitative
channel. Identification rests on MS/MS fragmentation of selected precursors, and
the MS1 signal is integrated afterward into extracted ion chromatograms for
label-free quantification. Yet the MS1 spectra record every peptide species that
ionizes, including the many that data-dependent acquisition never selects for
fragmentation, so each run carries identification-relevant evidence that a
fragment-centric analysis discards. Match-between-runs and identity-propagation
methods recover part of it by transferring identifications onto unfragmented
features @yu2021ionquant @xiao2025swaps.

Dropping MS/MS acquisition altogether takes this a step further. An instrument
method that records only MS1 spectra spends its full duty cycle on the survey
scan, which places more points across each chromatographic peak and supports
faster separations, both of which benefit quantification. The cost is the
fragment-ion evidence on which confident peptide identification normally rests.
Identification must instead come from what MS1 retains: accurate precursor mass
and retention time (RT). The accurate mass and time tag approach established
that this pair can identify peptides against a library of previously observed
coordinates @smith2002amt @zimmer2006amt. The same masses have also been used
within a single shotgun run, where the MS/MS identifications define the
candidate proteins and the unfragmented precursor masses add peptide evidence to
them @lu2008combining. Building on earlier MS/MS-free multi-enzyme work
@ivanov2017multienzyme, DirectMS1 removed the need for a prior MS/MS census,
assigning proteins from single short-gradient runs against an in silico database
@ivanov2020directms1 @ivanov2021ms1searchpy. The tradeoff is a weak
peptide-level signal: mass and RT alone produce many plausible matches, so
evidence must accumulate across multiple peptides at the protein level.

The reference implementation, ms1searchpy @ivanov2021ms1searchpy, combines a
mass search with DeepLC RT prediction @bouwmeester2021deeplc and learned
rescoring. A single-run search over that cascade is correspondingly expensive,
although the cost belongs to the workflow as a whole rather than to any one
stage of it. Its decoy shuffle and hyperparameter search also draw from
generators it does not expose, so repeated searches on one input return
different protein lists. That complicates high-throughput use, and it makes a
single search of either engine a weak basis for comparison.

The engineering problem is therefore not only to reproduce an identification
count. An MS1-only engine must search a large mass-compatible candidate space,
adapt a sequence RT predictor to an individual gradient, accumulate weak peptide
evidence at protein level, and expose enough intermediate structure to audit its
false discovery rate (FDR). Moving one stage into a faster language would leave
most of that workflow unchanged. uno instead implements the complete search
cascade in one Rust process and produces output that does not depend on input
order or thread scheduling.

We present uno, an open-source MS1-only identification engine written in Rust.
It reads the same single-run feature files as ms1searchpy and predicts RT
in-process. Like the comparator it draws on randomness, and holding its seed
fixed reproduces a run exactly. We evaluate the practical claim directly: on
matched feature files and databases, does uno provide greater externally
calibrated protein depth with less computation? The comparison uses datasets
from the DirectMS1 studies and an independent Orbitrap Astral series, with
two-proteome entrapment applied identically to both engines.

= Experimental Section <sec:methods>

== Input and pipeline position <sec:methods-input>

uno consumes a single-run feature table and a protein FASTA. Required feature
fields are neutral mass, $m slash z$, charge, and apex RT. Per-feature quality
descriptors from the feature finder, such as isotope-pattern fit, mass error,
and number of scans, are used when present. The main engine comparisons search
each run independently so that RT is calibrated in its own gradient space. The
benchmark gave uno and ms1searchpy the same feature table, FASTA, and digest,
isolating the search engines from feature detection and cross-run alignment.

uno can also skip the external table. With `--raw` it runs the koth feature
finder @koth in-process on an mzML, Bruker `.d`, or Thermo `.raw` file and
passes the scored features straight to the search. The bundled koth profile
keeps short, low-intensity mass traces (hills) so that its detected feature
count matches that of biosaur2 for this search. For acquisitions using
high-field asymmetric waveform ion mobility spectrometry (FAIMS), koth
partitions MS1 hill detection and feature assembly by compensation voltage
before concatenating the results. To measure the cost of the built-in path, the
six benchmark files and the #s(
  "astral.files",
) Astral files (#s("raw.dataset_count") in all) were searched a second time from
their converted mzML with `--raw`. The FASTA, RT model, and configuration were
held fixed, and the two protein lists were compared under the same entrapment
scorer (@sec:results-raw).

== Software architecture and data flow <sec:methods-architecture>

#figure(
  placement: auto,
  fig("fig.fig1-pipeline", width: 100%),
  caption: [
    uno's identification workflow. Dashed boxes are external inputs or tools,
    blue boxes are stages executed in one uno process, and the green box is the
    reported protein output. For the benchmark, biosaur2 performed feature
    detection and both engines received the identical single-run feature table.
  ],
) <fig:pipeline>

uno separates feature detection from identification (@fig:pipeline). This
boundary permits a controlled engine comparison and lets users substitute any
feature finder that supplies neutral mass, charge, apex RT, and optional quality
descriptors. Once the feature table and FASTA are loaded, digestion, candidate
retrieval, RT prediction, calibration, match scoring, and protein inference run
without exporting candidates to an external prediction service.

The theoretical peptide database is sorted by neutral mass. For each feature and
isotope-error hypothesis, a binary search locates the beginning of the mass
window, and scanning stops when peptide mass exceeds the window's upper
boundary. Candidates are reduced to the top-$K$ targets and top-$K$ decoys as
they are generated, so the complete peptide-feature match list is never held in
memory. This streaming step bounds the largest intermediate data structure while
preserving the candidates needed for RT discrimination.

RT predictions are computed for unique candidate sequences and mapped onto the
observed gradient before protein inference. The final protein table reports
accession and grouping information, observed and theoretical peptide counts, the
binomial score, and the internal $q$ value. The benchmark measured the complete
process end to end and did not assign the observed speed difference to any one
implementation stage.

== Peptide database and decoys <sec:methods-database>

sage-core @lazear2023sage performs in silico digestion, after which uno stores
peptides in a mass-sorted vector. Decoys are generated by pooling and shuffling
interior residues within each protein while retaining peptide termini. This
preserves organism-level composition while generally changing peptide sequence
and mass, so the null samples random mass coincidences rather than exact
target-decoy twins. Full digest and decoy settings are reported in Supporting
Information @sec:si-protocol.

== Feature-to-peptide matching <sec:methods-matching>

Each feature is matched to peptides within the mass tolerance at isotope-error
offsets of 0 and $plus.minus 1$ isotope peak (#lit("1.00335") Da, the mass
difference between carbon-13 and carbon-12). The top-$K$ target and decoy
candidates by mass error enter scoring, with $K$ set to 50 in the reported
configuration.

== Retention-time model <sec:methods-rt>

uno predicts RT with an embedded, pure-Rust convolutional neural network (CNN)
trained on the Chronologer harmonized library @wilburn2023chronologer. The
network combines learned sequence features with eleven physicochemical
descriptors. Training used sequences with fixed carbamidomethyl cysteine and no
other modifications, with sequence-disjoint training, validation, and holdout
sets. Architecture and training details are given in Supporting Information
@sec:si-rt-model.

The frozen model predicts a hydrophobic index rather than minutes. A monotone
calibration initialized by random sample consensus (RANSAC) @fischler1981ransac
maps this index to each run's gradient. Mass-supported protein identifications
initialize the mapping; subsequent scoring rounds refine it and the residual
scale using the current high-confidence set.

We compared the predictor with DeepLC #lit("3.1.13") @bouwmeester2021deeplc on
three public unmodified-peptide sets, excluding uno training and validation
sequences. Both models used identical affine calibration on 20% of each set,
with accuracy measured on the remaining 80%. Throughput was measured separately
on a synthetic tryptic library. Complete protocols are in Supporting Information
@sec:si-rt-predictor.

== Scoring and FDR <sec:methods-scoring>

Peptide-feature matches are ranked by recalibrated mass error and calibrated RT
deviation. Protein evidence is a binomial survival score,
$-log_10 P(X >= k | n, p)$, asking whether a protein's $k$ matched peptides out
of $n$ theoretical peptides exceed the decoy-estimated random-match rate $p$.
Greedy parsimony, target-decoy pair competition, and a monotone $(d + 1) \/ t$
FDR estimate, where $d$ and $t$ are the decoy and target counts above a
threshold, produce the reported protein list.

The engine selects a cutoff, from a fixed grid of values, on the sum of squared
standardized mass error and squared calibrated RT deviation. Selecting the most
permissive cutoff that maximizes accepted targets can bias an internal
target-decoy estimate, so the comparison does not use either engine's reported
FDR as its performance criterion. Instead, @sec:methods-benchmark applies the
same external entrapment calculation to both outputs.

A seeded, cross-validated gradient-boosted classifier @sequoiaboost can rerank
matches before protein inference. It uses the available feature quality
descriptors and is skipped when they are absent. The protein score remains the
binomial model.

All result-affecting random components draw from one selectable seed: the decoy
shuffle, the RANSAC calibration sample, the cross-validation fold assignment,
and the classifier's row and column subsampling. Values are sorted before
floating-point aggregation or tie-breaking. The default seed reproduces the
archived default runs. Varying the seed gives independent draws, which is how
the run-to-run variability in @sec:results-reproducibility was measured. A
two-run byte comparison on a complete feature file is part of the test suite.

== Benchmark data and comparison <sec:methods-benchmark>

The primary benchmark comprised five 5-minute HeLa Q Exactive HF runs from
PXD015669 and one Orbitrap Fusion Lumos FAIMS run from PXD023977, all used in
the DirectMS1 studies (file names as in Supporting Information @tbl:datasets).
ThermoRawFileParser @hulstaert2020thermorawfileparser converted each raw file
and biosaur2 @abdrakhimov2021biosaur detected features. Both engines received
the same feature table for each run.

Both engines searched the same combined human and #emph[S. cerevisiae] FASTA and
tryptic digest and generated decoys internally. The initial resource-measurement
set comprised one default-seed uno search and three ms1searchpy searches per
file, run one at a time on one workstation (hereafter serialized). Wall time and
peak resident memory were captured by `/usr/bin/time -v`. Depth, overlap, and
calibration were subsequently evaluated over #s("bench.uno_seed_count") uno
seeds and #s("bench.ms1_draw_min") to #s(
  "bench.ms1_draw_max",
) ms1searchpy runs per primary file. Versions, hardware, commands, files, and
per-run results are in Supporting Information @sec:si-protocol and
@sec:si-results.

Four defaults (the candidate cap `top_k`, the peptide-inclusion band averaged by
the classifier stage, the classifier's `min_child_weight`, and the
deviation-cutoff grid limit `cut_max`) were selected on two development
datasets, run10 and deephela (@tbl:datasets), by maximizing yeast-entrapment
depth. Neither development set is counted as an independent benchmark. The six
primary files and the Astral series were not used to select among reported
configurations.

The comparator was a patched ms1searchpy #lit("2.8")-series development state
that reads the feature quality descriptors available to uno. The exact patch is
part of the benchmark archive. This gave the comparator more information than
the stock release and therefore did not disadvantage it.

Depth is reported by two-proteome entrapment rather than by either engine's own
target/decoy estimate, using one scorer for both engines
(`benchmark/entrapment.py`). The sample is human and contains no yeast, so any
accepted yeast protein is a false positive. Ranking target proteins by score,
let $n_"human" (k)$ and $n_"yeast" (k)$ be the accepted counts of each species
at rank $k$, and $N_"human"$ and $N_"yeast"$ the number of entries of each in
the searched database. The scale-corrected false discovery proportion (FDP) at
rank $k$ is

$ "FDP"(k) = (n_"yeast" (k)) / (n_"human" (k)) dot (N_"human") / (N_"yeast") $

and the running minimum of this from the deepest cutoff upward gives $q$. Depth
at a threshold is the largest number of human proteins whose entrapment $q$
meets it. Depth in the main single-run engine comparisons is defined this way.
The matrix extension compares deduplicated accession lists at matched empirical
entrapment cutoffs and separately reports internal-threshold calibration
(@sec:si-matrix). Each engine's own reported 1% list was also scored against the
same entrapment proteome (@sec:results-calibration). The calculation assumes
that target and entrapment proteomes are exchangeable apart from database size
and therefore provides a bound rather than an unbiased FDR estimate
@wen2025entrapment.

Independent validation used #s("astral.files") HeLa Orbitrap Astral runs from
PXD066701, acquired in another laboratory on an 11-minute dual-column method
with a fixed FAIMS compensation voltage. Three 10 ng runs were selected as a
complete matched set before any search result was examined. After the 10 ng
result, we extended the comparison to the deposit's other input levels on the
same method, declaring the extension before any further file was searched. To
limit compute time, the extension was then restricted to 200 pg, 1 ng, and 100
ng, the first three files by name at each level, before any of the dropped
levels had been searched. Five 1 ng files had already been searched when that
restriction was made. The two beyond the first three by name were removed after
their results had been seen, so that every level has three files. Their
default-seed results remain in the benchmark archive: uno #lit(
  "567",
) and #lit("523") proteins at 1% entrapment FDR against #lit("768") to #lit(
  "975",
) on the three reported 1 ng files. Self-calibrated ms1searchpy returned #lit(
  "27",
) to #lit("39"), and the externally calibrated arm #lit("84") to #lit("197").
Both engines received identical biosaur2 feature tables, FASTA, and digest. uno
was initially run once per file, then extended to #s(
  "bench.extension_seed_count",
) seeds per file. ms1searchpy was run three times per file (ten on the 10 ng
files). These data were not used for model or parameter selection. Input-level
summaries are the median of the three file medians, with seeds or reruns
summarized within each file first. Additional uno searches ran concurrently and
contributed only depth and list measurements. Resource comparisons used the
serialized runs only.

The patched comparator run with its own two-stage DeepLC calibration, hereafter
self-calibrated ms1searchpy, failed to calibrate retention time on every one of
these files. Its second-stage DeepLC RT standard deviation ($sigma$) was #s(
  "astral.ms1_stock_rt_sigma_min",
) to #s(
  "astral.ms1_stock_rt_sigma_max",
) min on an 11-minute acquisition at every input level, wide enough that the RT
gate admitted most candidates. The calibration set it builds from its first-pass
proteins was dominated by random matches on this data, and none of its shipped
calibration options changed that (@sec:si-protocol). To separate this
calibration failure from the search itself, ms1searchpy was also run with its
documented `-pl` option, which calibrates DeepLC on a supplied list of
MS/MS-identified peptides. The list for each file was taken from the deposit's
own DIA-NN #lit("2.0.2") report, restricted to precursors at q #sym.lt.eq #lit(
  "0.01",
), unmodified and fully tryptic, giving #s("astral.pl_list_min") to #s(
  "astral.pl_list_max",
) peptides per file. In the tested ms1searchpy version, `-pl` with DeepLC raised
an exception. A one-line fix that keeps its two-stage path enabled is part of
the benchmark archive. This arm was run three times per file. uno received no
such list. The 10 ng runs of this arm were not serialized, so their timing is
not reported.

Low input is where the search is most seed-sensitive. Every stage of its
self-calibration cascade selects between alternatives by the number of proteins
at 1% internal FDR. That count is a step function on short lists, so at low
input a small change in the estimated RT $sigma$ can move a whole stage between
zero and a hundred proteins. Three safeguards address it. First, the pipeline
runs at two RT $sigma$ values, the estimate and #lit("1.5") times the estimate,
and keeps the candidate whose final list has the larger soft depth. Soft depth
is $sum_i max(0, 1 - q_i \/ 0.05)$ over the target proteins $i$, where $q_i$ is
the internal $q$ value. Second, when no cutoff on the combined mass and RT
deviation accepts a protein at the internal 1% threshold, the cutoff with the
largest classifier training set (targets at internal $q <= 0.25$) is retained
instead of an empty list. The later stages are then re-initialized from the
proteins identified by mass alone. A candidate that supplies only a training set
never displaces one that reports identifications. Third, if the complete
pipeline still reports no target at 1%, one further RT calibration pass runs on
the broad feature-level evidence, with no lower bound imposed on its RT $sigma$
estimate. That pass is accepted only if it identifies targets. Otherwise the
empty result stands. The second and third safeguards do no work on a search that
succeeds, and the first costs a second pass through the classifier stage.

The main single-run comparison searches were executed by one build, pinned by
commit and binary hash in Supporting Information @tbl:versions and recorded per
search in `benchmark/results/publication_rerun/`. The re-execution inventory is
given in @sec:si-reproducibility. The matrix extension uses a separately frozen
koth #lit("0.7.0") build and three seeds per route (@sec:si-matrix). Other
raw-mode and IM results retain their separately recorded software versions.

Threshold sensitivity was evaluated on the complete ranked protein lists at
entrapment-FDR cutoffs of #lit("0.5"), #lit("1"), #lit("2"), #lit("3"), #lit(
  "5",
) and #lit("10")%. At each cutoff and on each primary dataset, the median uno
depth was divided by the median ms1searchpy depth across the available draws.
The complete ratios are archived in `paper/figdata/threshold_sensitivity.tsv`.
The two conventional operating points are shown in @tbl:variability.

= Results and Discussion <sec:results>

== Identification depth and computational efficiency <sec:results-headtohead>

At 1% entrapment FDR, uno's median identification depth exceeded the ms1searchpy
median on #s("bench.uno_median_wins") of the #s("bench.dataset_count")
DirectMS1-study datasets (@fig:headtohead). The relative differences between
medians ranged from #s("bench.median_margin_1pct_min") to #s(
  "bench.median_margin_1pct_max",
)%. At 5% entrapment FDR, uno's median was higher on #s(
  "bench.uno_median_wins_5pct",
) datasets (@tbl:variability). On the two files where the ms1searchpy median was
higher at 1%, #lit("09746") and #lit("09766"), ms1searchpy fell below uno at
#lit("2")% and at every wider cutoff
(`paper/figdata/threshold_sensitivity.tsv`). These are descriptive comparisons
of repeated searches on identical input.

uno completed the searches in #s("bench.uno_wall_min") to #s(
  "bench.uno_wall_max",
) s, compared with #s("bench.ms1_wall_min") to #s("bench.ms1_wall_max") s for
ms1searchpy. The per-dataset speed advantage was #s("bench.speed_factor_min") to
#s("bench.speed_factor_max")-fold, with about half the peak resident memory.
Complete per-run values are in @tbl:perdataset.

