== Identification from LFQ matrices <sec:results-matrix>

uno can search an aligned LFQ matrix by treating each consensus precursor row as
one feature and retaining its per-run intensities for quantification. With fresh
koth #lit("0.7.0") features and LFQ, consensus-row searching recovered a median
#s("matrix.astral_consensus_median") human accessions in the Astral #lit("100")
ng triplicate, versus #s("matrix.astral_union_median") for the union of
individual searches at a matched empirical entrapment FDP of at most #lit("1")%.
The #lit("20")-run IonStar comparison also favored the individual-search union:
#s("matrix.ionstar_union_median") versus #s("matrix.ionstar_consensus_median")
present-species accessions. IonStar matrix quantities recovered a median _E.
coli_ highest-to-lowest concentration ratio of #s(
  "matrix.ecoli_ratio_consensus",
) against the expected #lit("3"). Direct matrix input therefore supported
identification and quantification, with reduced identification depth in these
two cohorts. The three-seed comparison and conditional quantitative checks are
reported in @sec:si-matrix.

= Discussion and limitations <sec:discussion>

The controlled comparison supports a practical, scoped advantage: on identical
feature input, uno had greater median entrapment-controlled depth on most
primary files and lower measured resource use. Entrapment is important to that
statement because neither engine's internal target-decoy estimate defines the
performance endpoint, and the depth comparison is between distributions rather
than between single runs, since both engines are randomized.

The architecture and agreement analyses clarify what that comparison does and
does not show. Mass-sorted streaming candidate retrieval, in-process RT
prediction, and protein-level evidence aggregation form one application rather
than a collection of separately timed components. The end-to-end benchmark is
therefore relevant to a user choosing an engine, but it cannot identify which
stage accounts for the runtime difference. Conversely, the overlap analysis
shows substantial common protein identity, alongside changes in which proteins
pass the threshold across seeds in both engines.

Matrix input searches each aligned precursor row as one feature and retains a
vector of sample quantities. The lower accession depth in both cohorts does not
support an identification advantage over separate searches. These datasets
informed development, and the focused comparison does not establish performance
at lower inputs or across cohort sizes.

The evidence does not establish universal superiority. The clearest boundary is
low input. At 200 pg and 1 ng, one file searched under different seeds returns
lists that differ by a large fraction of its median depth. The safeguards in
@sec:methods-benchmark keep such searches from returning nothing, but they do
not narrow that spread. At those loadings uno should be treated as an
exploratory tool whose depth is a distribution rather than a measurement. In the
head-to-head engine comparisons, all samples are HeLa and all gradients are
short. The Astral series represents one single-compensation-voltage acquisition
method. The built-in feature finder was compared only with biosaur2 and only on
these files. It is offered as a convenience with measured cost, not as a
feature-finder benchmark. The primary parameters were tuned on related
development data, although not on the reported benchmark or Astral files. The
six primary files are a dataset panel rather than a population sample, so the
per-dataset ratios and seed or rerun ranges are descriptive. No population-level
significance is claimed. The Astral and raw-mode extension used only #s(
  "bench.extension_seed_count",
) seeds per file, which provides a limited sample of search variability. The
patched comparator received additional feature quality descriptors, which makes
the comparison conservative with respect to those inputs but means the reported
timings and depths do not describe an unmodified public release.

In addition, the entrapment FDP is quantized by a small number of accepted
entrapment proteins and depends on exchangeability between the target and
entrapment proteomes. The running-minimum $q$ construction can make a single
yeast hit affect a range of neighboring cutoffs, especially at #lit("0.5")% FDR.
A size-matched entrapment proteome, additional entrapment species, and a wider
acquisition panel would sharpen both absolute calibration and generalizability.
Generality of the head-to-head engine advantage on non-HeLa samples and longer
gradients remains unresolved.

A fixed seed is an operational convenience rather than evidence of a better
search. It lets a result be regression-tested and compared across releases
without confusing a code change with a new random draw, and combined with
external FDR calibration it makes the benchmark auditable. It is not a property
that distinguishes the engines, since the comparator's randomness is seedable
too. The distinguishing claims here are depth, resource use, and calibration.

= Conclusions <sec:conclusions>

uno is a fast Rust engine for MS1-only protein identification. It combines
substantially lower measured computation with higher median
entrapment-controlled depth on most tested DirectMS1 files and on the
independent Astral input series. At 200 pg and 1 ng its depth still varies
strongly with the seed, and one search of the built-in feature-detection arm
returned nothing. Depth and protein identity vary across seeds, so a fixed seed
ensures repeatability rather than a uniformly better search. Its internally
reported FDR is not uniformly calibrated, so the claim is deliberately limited
to performance under the common entrapment criterion.

